@MOL
Holst, of course, wrote only eight movements--not because he snubbed Pluto's planetary status, but because the object hadn't been discovered yet. Also, he was inspired by the astrological connotations of the planets, not astronomy per se, nor did he have them in order from the sun. I'm not even sure he thought of them as a unified "suite," at least at first. 

Several composers since have written "Pluto" addenda, none of them very good. Unlikely to get many more performances, nowadays.

@Bill
I love "The Planets" and always assumed Holst arranged them in order of  dramatic sequence.  PhysicsDaughter, age 5, was excited by what she was hearing, and DHubby explained to her that the composer wanted the music to sound like the warlike planet.  (This turned into an explanation of what planets were-sorta-- and I agreed to take her to the library for a book.)  So, we get to the Children's Room and PhysDau makes her request; the sweet librarian says, "Oh, plants!...Are you going to have a garden?"  Our never-patient daughter exclaims, "Not PLANTS!  PLAN-ETS!  Like Mars, The Bringer of War!"  
